Debug and sanitizer builds
==========================

lib/configure honours three environment variables.  All of them are unset by
default, so a plain "./configure" produces exactly the same configure.mak as
before.

  EXTRA_CFLAGS    appended to CFLAGS.  Also reaches the linker, because
                  LDFLAGS starts with $(CFLAGS).
  EXTRA_LDFLAGS   appended to LDFLAGS only.
  STRIPFLAG       link-time strip flag, "-s" by default.  Set it empty to
                  keep symbols.

Pass them to make, not to a separate ./configure run.  The "all" target in
lib/Makefile re-runs configure on every build, so flags handed to configure
directly are overwritten again a moment later.  Putting them in make's
environment works because make passes its environment on to the recipe that
runs configure:

  EXTRA_CFLAGS='...' STRIPFLAG= make

configure.h and configure.mak are generated files (see .gitignore), so
switching between build flavours leaves no trace in the working tree.


UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan)
----------------------------------

This is the one that has actually paid off on this code base.  The LP64
conversion (int32 = int instead of long) turned a number of previously
harmless 32-bit computations into signed overflow, which is undefined
behaviour: the compiler is allowed to assume it never happens and optimise
accordingly.  UBSan reports each occurrence at run time with file, line and
the offending values.

Interactive build, for a test run with a terminal attached:

  make clean
  EXTRA_CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=undefined' \
  STRIPFLAG= make

Findings are printed to stderr and the program keeps running.  That is what
you want while testing by hand.

For CI, make the first finding fatal, otherwise the job exits 0 and the
diagnostics scroll past unread:

  EXTRA_CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all' \
  STRIPFLAG= make

Back to a normal build:

  make clean && make

The "make clean" matters in both directions: the flags only affect newly
compiled objects, and a tree half built with and half without instrumentation
will not link.

Do not ship a sanitizer build.  It costs roughly 20% run time, and on a
daemon whose stderr goes nowhere it buys you nothing (see below).


Where the output goes
---------------------

UBSan writes to stderr.  It never creates files and needs no permissions
unless you ask it to.

Note that dobye() in src/remote_net.c does

    freopen("/dev/null", "r+", stderr);

when the console is released, so after a "bye" the diagnostics are silently
discarded.  Run the sanitizer build from a terminal (or under systemd, where
stderr lands in the journal) and do not detach the console.

If you ever do need a file, UBSan can open one itself, bypassing whatever the
program does to its file descriptors:

  UBSAN_OPTIONS=log_path=/var/log/wampes-ubsan,print_stacktrace=1

That writes to /var/log/wampes-ubsan.<pid> and needs a writable directory.


What UBSan does not find
------------------------

  - Buffer overflows into otherwise valid memory.  Writing past the end of an
    mbuf into the next heap object is perfectly defined as far as UBSan is
    concerned.  That is AddressSanitizer territory - see the caveat below.
  - Reads of uninitialised memory.  That is MemorySanitizer
    (-fsanitize=memory, clang on Linux only, needs every library
    instrumented) or valgrind.
  - Logic and authentication flaws.  No sanitizer will ever flag those.

UBSan is also a run-time tool: it only sees code paths that actually execute.
A live run over normal radio traffic exercises the normal paths; the error
paths that an attacker aims at are never reached on their own.  Feeding the
parsers directly (ntohdomain(), slhc_uncompress(), ntohax25(), slip_decode(),
ntohntp()) is the way to cover those - they take a buffer or an mbuf chain
and need no network setup.


AddressSanitizer: does not work as-is
-------------------------------------

Do not enable -fsanitize=address without reading this.

src/kernel.c implements its own cooperative scheduler and switches stacks
with getcontext()/makecontext()/swapcontext() (or, where HAS_UCONTEXT is 0,
with the hand-written setstack() in src/setsp.c).  ASan tracks stack extents
in a shadow map and does not cope with stacks being switched behind its back.
The result is invented stack-buffer-overflow and stack-use-after-return
reports, or a crash inside the sanitizer itself.

Making it work requires wrapping every context switch in
__sanitizer_start_switch_fiber() / __sanitizer_finish_switch_fiber().  Until
that is done, use ASan only on standalone test drivers that do not start the
WAMPES scheduler.

UBSan has no such problem - it does not care which stack you are on.


32-bit builds and the private allocator
---------------------------------------

src/alloc.c uses its own allocator unless PURIFY, __NeXT__, __NetBSD__,
__sgi, __MACOSX__ or __x86_64__ is defined.  On i386 the private allocator is
active: WAMPES grabs large blocks from malloc() and carves them up itself, so
a memory checker only sees the blocks and is blind to overruns between WAMPES
objects.

For i386 test builds add -DPURIFY to EXTRA_CFLAGS to force the system
allocator.


Compiler warnings without building
----------------------------------

A syntax-only pass is cheap and catches the format-string and prototype
problems without linking anything:

  cd src
  for f in *.c; do cc -fsyntax-only -Wall -Wformat -I. -I../lib $f; done

-Wformat catches printf type mismatches, which matter again now that int32 is
32 bits wide.  It cannot see the arithmetic problems; that is what UBSan is
for.
